#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180395} :activity/id #uuid "e609e5db-3168-57af-8ec3-6779df22874b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050666 :topic/id #uuid "5d68aacb-ca27-5530-ae69-05289931b444" :topic/math-academy-id 3705} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180394 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179675 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179531 :question/id #uuid "7cec344e-596a-51ce-87cc-d0e44dc48df4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7118"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3be2c44e-775f-53fa-a078-cff25eaea903" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051000}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535023}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"} :step/id #uuid "1221b846-bf07-5b93-bf5a-5f8ba64ac365" :step/math-academy-id 26175 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180477}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180395 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180681 :tutorial/content "Line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y$ often come in pairs. For example, \n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C (x+y) \\, \\textrm dx + \\int\\limits_C (x-y) \\, \\textrm dy.\n$$\n\nWe usually express a sum of line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y$ as a single integral. In the case above, we can write this sum as\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C (x+y) \\, \\textrm dx + (x-y) \\, \\textrm dy.\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate this integral in this case where $C$ is given by the line segment\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\underbrace{(6t+1)}_{x(t)} \\,\\mathbf{i} + \\underbrace{(2t+1)}_{y(t)} \\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad t\\in\\left[0,1\\right].\n$$\n\nAlong the path $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = 6t+1, \\qquad y = 2t+1.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1)\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the line integral by applying a change of variables, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x+(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((x+y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t+1+2t+1)\\cdot 6+(6t+1-(2t+1))\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\cdot 6+(4t)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}48t+12+8t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}56t+12\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={28{t}^{2}+12t|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =28+12 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7c3e84f3-cbc8-5522-87f2-5accaa3854e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4437 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "621fd314-933c-56a6-97f4-7a591c8ceee3" :step/math-academy-id 27289 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180394}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180476 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179729 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179591 :question/id #uuid "2b0b2d1f-df55-56cf-9ae9-ccef499e2352" :question/math-academy-id "e-9517"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5ecc45-95ec-55c0-86bf-bbaeffef4049" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535072}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X, Y, and Z"} :step/id #uuid "3d3c67b4-ef9a-5648-81cf-8ed48d14fa68" :step/math-academy-id 35620 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180487}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180477 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180712 :tutorial/content "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function of three variables and $C$ be a path in three-dimensional space. Suppose we want to calculate the following sum of line integrals:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f \\, \\textrm dx + \\int\\limits_C f \\, \\textrm dy +  \\int\\limits_C f \\, \\textrm dz\n$$\n\nFor notational simplicity, we can express this as a single integral, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f \\, \\textrm dx + f \\, \\textrm dy +   f \\, \\textrm dz\n$$\n\nAs before, we can evaluate this integral by parameterizing the curve $C$ using the parameter $t\\in [a,b]$ and then rewriting the integral using a change of variables, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f\\,\\text{d}x+f\\,\\text{d}y+f\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+f\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+f\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(f\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+f\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+f\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we often omit the large parentheses and simply write\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + f \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} +   f \\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t} \\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nFinally, it can be helpful to switch to prime notation for the derivatives when dealing with complex integrands. In this case, the above formula becomes\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f x'+ f y' +   f z' \\,\\textrm d t,\n$$\n\nwhere the primes denote differentiation with respect to $t.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8db52e8b-f669-5f77-b902-168c59d3b98f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5813 :tutorial/title "Line Integrals With Respect to X, Y, and Z"} :step/id #uuid "643f2b27-0aa4-53bd-995f-f4862424f404" :step/math-academy-id 35621 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180476}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179734 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179596 :question/id #uuid "c18fbc4a-24d5-517d-9a3c-949a11787076" :question/math-academy-id "e-9528"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2daf57e1-a02d-5e5f-b4a4-bb8ae9638a75" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535057}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral"} :step/id #uuid "d3d36521-e553-55b3-9ce0-83868054277e" :step/math-academy-id 35687}] :activity/title "Sums of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Over Parametric Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179858}]}
